.DS_Store

# ── Source material for the cinematic templates ──
# Saved copies of the licensed source pages, kept locally for reference. Only
# the artwork is ported (into frontend/public/templates/<key>/); the folders
# themselves also carry vendor tracking bundles — a 566KB Google tag bundle and
# a 410KB Facebook pixel — which must never reach the repo or a guest's
# invitation. See test/swanLakeTemplate.test.jsx.
#
# THE LEADING SLASH IS LOAD-BEARING. A bare `templates/` is not "the templates
# folder at the root" — it matches a directory of that name at ANY depth, and
# this repo has two more that matter:
#     frontend/src/app/components/templates/   the entire template component tree
#     frontend/public/templates/               every template's artwork
# Written unanchored, git silently refused to track new files under both. The
# tree still built locally and the already-tracked files were unaffected, so
# nothing looked wrong until the deploy failed on a missing module.
# `/templates/` anchors the match to this file's own directory.
/templates/
/envelop/
/envelop1/

# ── Screenshot scratch ──
# Where test/shots/*.dump.jsx stages HTML and the headless-Chrome captures
# land. Regenerable from the harness (which IS committed) and several MB of
# PNG per pass, so the output stays out. Anchored, for the reason above.
/.visual/

# ── Android ──
# Machine-specific: sdk.dir, and the release signing credentials.
android/local.properties
android/**/build/
android/.gradle/

# Signing keys. A keystore committed even once is compromised permanently —
# git history keeps it after the file is deleted, and this key cannot be
# rotated: Android identifies an app by its signing key, so a replacement key
# produces an app that cannot upgrade an installed one.
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12

# The built app. `deploy-android.bat :install` DELETES this and scp's a fresh
# 46MB copy down from the build host on every deploy, so each run committed
# another 46MB blob — none of which git can ever reclaim. It is a build output:
# the artefact users actually install is published to /var/www/apk on the VPS,
# and this copy exists only to feed `adb install`.
#
# Anchored, for the reason given above `/templates/`.
/app-release.apk

# ⚠ INERT AS WRITTEN — the quotes are literal.
#
# git does not strip quotation marks from a pattern, so this asks it to ignore
# a path whose name begins with a `"` character. Nothing matches, and 179 files
# under images/ are tracked today. Left exactly as found rather than "fixed":
# correcting the quoting would start ignoring that folder, and since the files
# already tracked stay tracked either way, the only thing it would change is
# silently refusing NEW ones — the same failure the `/templates/` note above
# describes. Decide what images/ is for, then write the rule deliberately.
"/images/"
